# Model upstream revision

What constructing a bounded prototype proves wrong in settings and any space units it consumes, and what is repaired there. Every selected model H2 answers for its actual parents.

## Settings and space revision from model work {#settings-and-space-revision-from-model-work}

Model work tests whether its subject can be represented within the declared capabilities, scale, contacts, and spatial interfaces. Report the exact parent defect it exposes: contradictory extents, an impossible articulation or change limit, an unnamed contact or attachment, a space interface that cannot accept the subject, or a required visible state with no settings owner. Repair that parent before constructing again; source geometry and a local prototype exception may not invent the missing authority.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the construction decision that exposed it. When this unit exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names the concrete capability, scale, contact, space, and state decisions examined and why they were sufficient. A parentless unit or repeated "parents were adequate" answer covers nothing.

Review question: what did constructing this model unit prove wrong in its actual settings or space parents, or which concrete parent decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [glTF 2.0 on explicit node, mesh, skin, and animation interfaces](https://registry.khronos.org/glTF/specs/2.0/glTF-2.0.html); [NASA on bidirectional traceability and correction at the responsible level](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)
